const pageTitle = "Child Development Care & Developmental Treatment for Children in India";
const pageSubtitle = "Supporting Healthy Brain Development, Learning, Behaviour & Growth Through Every Stage of Childhood";
const articleRaw = "Introduction\nKey Takeaways\nUnderstanding Child Development & Developmental Milestones\nImportance of Early Development & Identification\nBrain, Cognitive, Speech & Language Development\nSocial, Emotional & Behavioural Development\nAutism, ADHD & Developmental Delays\nLearning, Attention & Executive Function Challenges\nSensory Processing & Motor Development\nSigns Parents Should Not Ignore\nComprehensive Child Development Assessment\nNeuro-Ayurveda Development System\nPersonalised Developmental Support & Guidance\nParent Education & Family Involvement\nLong-Term Development Monitoring & Follow-Up\nFAQs About Child Development Care\n\n\nSupporting Healthy Brain Development, Learning, Behaviour & Growth Through Every Stage of Childhood\n\n\nChild Development Care & Developmental Treatment for Children in India\n\nAll children develop at different rates. Some children develop their age-appropriate skills spontaneously, while others might require extra time and/or assistance in a variety of areas, including communication, learning, behaviour, attention, movement or social interaction. These differences can be understood at an early stage and can be considered by parents to help their child in general development. [1][2]\n\nDevelopment of children is not only physical. It reflects how a child thinks, learns, communicates, behaves, moves and relates to the world around them. These various stages of development are interrelated and affect a child's confidence, independence and quality of life. [1]\n\nMany parents start to notice that their children have delayed speech, trouble holding their attention, behaviour problems, poor eye contact, learning problems, emotional outbursts or delayed developmental milestones. There are some normal variations, but if they are continuing then, developmental concerns are not to be overlooked. [2]\n\nEarly identification helps families to know and understand their child's strengths and where there might be a need for extra help. Each child is unique and early guidance can offer more opportunities to develop children's communication, learning and everyday skills. [1][2]\n\nAt Manovaidya, we know that there are many complex factors that affect child development such as brain development, nutrition, gut health, sleep, sensory processing, behaviour, emotional wellbeing and the child's environment. We do not make judgements about children based on one symptom or diagnosis, but rather, we strive to gain a full developmental picture of each child. [4]\n\nUnder the guidance of Dr. Ankush Garg, Autism, ADHD, Child Development & Mental Health Specialist in India, Manovaidya provides children with a structured five-pillar approach known as Neuro-Ayurveda Development System to make parents aware of the developmental needs of their child through detailed assessment, customized directions and holistic developmental assistance.\n\nAt Manovaidya, Child Development Care Focuses On:\n\nChild Development Assessment\nDevelopmental Delays\nAutism Spectrum Treatment\nADHD Treatment\nSpeech & Language Development\nLearning & Academic Challenges\nBehavioural Development\nEmotional Development\nSocial Skills Development\nSensory Processing Support\nMotor Development\nParent Guidance & Family Support\n\nUnderstanding Child Development\n\nChild development is a process that starts before birth and extends through childhood and adolescence. Along the way children become increasingly physically capable, communicative, emotionally regulated, thinking and socially related and independent. [1]\n\nDevelopmental milestones are a general guideline for the age at which many children are able to meet various developmental abilities; however, all children will have different developmental progressions. Small differences will occur. However, if delays are significant or persistent, it may be useful for a child to be further assessed and supported. [2]\n\nHealthy child development involves several interconnected domains:\n\nBrain and cognitive development\nLanguage and speech skills\nSocial communication\nEmotional regulation\nBehavioural development\nFine and Gross motor skills\nSensory processing\nLearning and attention\nDaily living skills\n\nImprovements in one field can impact upon improvements in another. For instance, difficulties with communication can impact social relationships, sensory processing differences can impact behaviour, attention and/or participation in learning activities.\n\nMany parents looking for Child Development Assessment, Child Development Delay Support, Autism Treatment, Speech Delay Guidance, ADHD Treatment and Child Development Care in India are looking for answers and understanding as to why their child is developing differently. The early identification of difficulties is a key initial step in giving the right guidance and support.\n\nWhy Early Child Development Matters\n\nThe early years of life are a period of rapid brain development. Children learn during this time, through experiences, relationships, play, communication and interaction with their environment. [1]\n\nSupporting development in these early years can facilitate children to reinforce essential life skills including:\n\nCommunication\nLearning\nEmotional regulation\nSocial interaction\nProblem-solving\nAttention\nIndependence\nBehavioural flexibility\n\nEarly identification does not equate to labelling a child. Rather, it offers a chance to gain insight into the child's learning, communication and world experiences. [1][2]\n\nParents can identify developmental issues early on and apply the right direction and guidance, and establish environments that promote learning and development.\n\nEvery Child Develops Across Multiple Areas\n\nChild development is so more than just making it to milestones. All areas of development impact on children's learning, communication and interaction with others.\n\nBrain & Cognitive Development\n\nFacilitates thinking, memory, attention, problem solving, learning.\n\nSpeech & Language Development\n\nEnables the language to be understood and ideas and information to be communicated.\n\nEmotional Development\n\nAcknowledges, expresses and regulates emotions in an appropriate manner with children.\n\nSocial Development\n\nEncourages friendships, co-operation, sharing, empathy and social communication.\n\nMotor Development\n\nCovers gross motor skills (e.g. running, jumping, balancing) and fine motor skills (e.g. writing, drawing, buttoning clothes).\n\nSensory Processing\n\nAssists children to react to sounds, touch, movement, textures, light and other forms of sensory response appropriately.\n\nBehavioural Development\n\nComprehends and regulates emotions, adjusts to various settings, is flexible.\n\nAttention & Executive Function\n\nHelps plan, organise, control impulses, concentrate, remember and perform daily activities.\n\n\nCommon Child Development Concerns\n\nAll children learn at their own speed. Development is subject to some transitory changes but for some children the problems are ongoing and impact on communication, learning, behaviour, movement or social interaction. [2]\n\n\nIf these concerns are identified early, parents can find the right guidance and offer support when it is needed most, as the individual needs of their child are taken into account.\n\nIn Manovaidya we focus not on individual symptoms or diagnoses, but on the child's overall developmental profile. All children are individual and support is tailored to their strengths and developmental needs.\n\nAutism Spectrum Disorder (ASD)\n\nA number of children have difficulties with social communication and behaviour, play patterns and sensory processing, which are similar to those seen in Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD). [3]\n\nChildren with autism may have problems with:\n\nLimited eye contact\nDelayed communication\nRepetitive behaviours\nDifficulty with social interaction\nSensory sensitivities\nPreference for routines\n\nAll children with autism are different and each child has their own range of abilities. [3]\n\n→ Learn More About Autism Treatment\n\nAttention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD)\n\nChildren with ADHD may have persistent problems concentrating, controlling their impulses or being hyperactive that interfere with learning, behavior, and functioning.\n\nSome children may:\n\nFind it difficult to stay focused\nBe easily distracted\nAct impulsively\nHave difficulty sitting still\nForget instructions\nStruggle with organisation\n\n\nThese behaviours can be assessed by professionals to ascertain if they are associated with ADHD or developmental issues.\n\n\n→ Learn More About ADHD Treatment\n\nSpeech & Language Delay\n\nAll children have different communication skills.\n\nSome children may:\n\nUse less vocabulary than usual\nHave difficulty understanding language\nHas difficulty with expressing needs\nUse unclear speech\nHave delayed language milestones\n\nEarly evaluation can be used to learn about communication skills that are strong and those that need strengthening. [1][2]\n\n→ Learn More About Speech & Language Development\n\nLearning Difficulties\n\nSome children have difficulties learning to read and write, have problems in mathematics, or are unable to understand the instructions in the classroom, although they are provided with regular educational opportunities.\n\nLearning difficulties can impact on:\n\nAcademic performance\nConfidence\nMotivation\nClassroom participation\nDaily learning activities\n\nA knowledge of a child's learning process enables parents and teachers to offer the right education.\n\nA request for information regarding learning difficulties.Request for information on learning difficulties.\n\nDevelopmental Delay\n\nSome children meet their developmental goals at later ages in one or more developmental areas.\n\nA developmental delay can occur in the development of:\n\nCommunication\nMovement\nLearning\nSocial interaction\nSelf-care skills\nCognitive development\n\nA developmental assessment is used to understand the child's current developmental skills, and to identify areas that need support. [2]\n\n→ Learn More About Developmental Delay\n\nBehavioural Challenges\n\nChildren can communicate their needs through their behaviour.\n\nSome children may experience:\n\nFrequent tantrums\nDifficulty managing emotions\nAggressive behaviour\nDifficulty following routines\nImpulsivity\nEmotional outbursts\n\nTo assist healthy development, it is crucial that reasons for behaviour are understood.\n\nThe learner will gain an understanding of behaviour development.\n\nSensory Processing Differences\n\nMany children react in a unique way to common stimuli like sounds, touch, movement, textures or lights.\n\nThey may:\nAvoid certain textures\nBecome overwhelmed by loud noises\nSeek excessive movement\nBe unusually sensitive to clothing\nHave difficulty adapting to busy environments\nSensory processing differences can affect behavior, learning and participation in everyday activities.\n→ Learn More About Sensory Processing Support\n\nMotor Development Delay\n\nMotor development involves gross motor skills as well as fine motor skills.\n\nChildren may have problems with:\n\nCrawling\nWalking\nRunning\nBalance\nHandwriting\nHolding objects\nButtoning clothes\n\nMotor skills are an integral part of independence and engagement in daily life.\n→ Learn More About Motor Development Support\nSocial Communication Difficulties\n\nSome children have problems understanding and taking part in normal social interaction.\n\nThey might have difficulties with:\n\nMaking friends\nSharing interests\nUnderstanding emotions\nTaking turns\nStarting conversations\nUnderstanding social situations\n\nSocial communication skills are acquired over time, and can be supported in a way that has a positive impact if problems are detected early.\n\nFor more information about Social Skills Development, see the links below.\n\nEmotional Regulation\n\nIt's a major developmental step to grasp and regulate emotions.\n\nSome children may:\n\nBecome frustrated easily\nCry frequently\nHave intense emotional reactions\nFind it difficult to calm themselves\nStruggle with transitions\n\nEmotional Regulation support contributes to children's confidence, resilience and healthy relationships.\n\nTime spent on activities that involve emotional development.Time spent on activities that contain emotional learning.\n\nExecutive Function Challenges\n\nExecutive functions assist children in planning, organizing and remembering instructions and handling daily tasks.\n\nThere may be issues for some children with:\n\nPlanning activities\nCompleting tasks\nFollowing multi-step instructions\nTime management\nOrganisation\nSelf-control\n\nExecutive function skills develop during childhood and adolescence.\n\n→ Learn More About Executive Function Support\n\nDevelopmental Milestones\n\nDevelopmental milestones are general guidelines to the skills that many children attain by a certain age. Some variation is normal as each child will develop at his or her own rate. If there are continuing delays or regression in skills, however, it should be reviewed with a trained health care worker. [1][2]\n\nBirth to 6 Months\n\nMost infants start to:\n\nMake eye contact\nSmile responsively\nTurn towards familiar voices\nRaise their head when they are tummy time\nUse your hands to grab something close by\nRespond to sounds\n\n6 to 12 Months\n\nMost babies start to:\n\nSit without support\nCrawl\nBabble using different sounds\nWhen called, answer their name.Answer when they call.\nDemonstrate curiosity through play\nUse simple gestures such as waving, smiling, nodding, etc. to communicate your ideas.\n\n1 to 2 Years\n\nMost young children start to:\n\nWalk independently\nEmphasise the use of simple words and short phrases.\nFollow simple instructions\nPoint to objects that are familiar.\nEnjoy pretend play\nShow interest in interacting with others\n\n2 to 3 Years\n\nBy the time they're young, many kids start to:\n\nSpeak in short sentences\nRecognize familiar people and things\nRun, climb and jump\nFollow simple routines\nPlay together and interact with one another.\nExpress basic emotions\n\n3 to 5 Years\n\nMost children will start to:\n\nSpeak more clearly\nAsk questions\nPlay cooperatively with other children\nDraw simple shapes\nFollow multi-step instructions\nDevelop greater independence\n\n5 to 8 Years\n\nChildren develop in most cases:\n\nUnderstanding of reading and writing skills.\nAttention\nProblem-solving\nEmotional regulation\nFriendships\nSelf-care skills\nAcademic learning\n\nSigns Parents Should Not Ignore\n\nWhile each child grows at his or her own pace, parents should seek professional evaluation if they have concerns that are ongoing, including: [2]\n\nCommunication\n\nLimited speech development\nLoss of the language that has been learned.\nPoor ability to follow simple directions\nLimited eye contact\n\nLearning\n\nDifficulty learning new information\nDifficulty listening to and participating in classroom activities\nSignificant academic struggles\nWhen students' attention is not focused during learning.\n\nBehaviour\n\nFrequent emotional outbursts\nSignificant challenges with transitions\nPersistent impulsive behaviour\nAggressive behaviour that interferes with daily life\n\nSocial Interaction\n\nLittle desire to communicate with others\nDifficulty making friends\nLack of shared play\nExhibits poor response to social cues.Has restricted social cue responses.\n\nMovement\n\nWhen a child struggles to walk or run.When children are slow to walk or run.\nPoor balance\nLimited ability to use hands for daily activities\nDelayed motor milestones\n\nSensory Responses\n\nExtreme sensitivity to sounds, touch or textures\nUnusual sensory-seeking behaviours\nMay struggle to function in noisy settings.Can't handle crowded spaces well.\n\nDaily Living Skills\n\nDifficulty eating independently\nChallenges with dressing or toileting appropriate for developmental age\nDifficulty following everyday routines\n\nRecognising these signs early does not necessarily mean a child has a specific condition. They simply indicate that a comprehensive developmental assessment may help understand the child's strengths, needs and appropriate next steps. [2]\n\n\nDifferent areas of child development including speech learning behaviour and motor skills\n\nOur Child Development Assessment Process\n\nEach child is unique in strengths and challenges, and in the way they learn. We feel that it is more important to understand the whole child than just one behaviour or diagnosis at Manovaidya.\n\nBecause symptoms are not unique, our process of assessing a child considers several factors within a child's development to create a more comprehensive profile of the child's development. [2]\n\nAssessment serves to highlight strengths, identify developmental needs and support parents to understand the best next steps for their children. [2]\n\nStep 1 - Parent Consultation\n\nEvery assessment begins with listening to the parents. \n\nWe take time to understand:\n\nParent's observations and concerns\nPregnancy and birth history\nDevelopmental milestones\nMedical history\nSchool performance\nDaily routines\nBehaviour at home and school\nPrevious assessments or therapies, (if any)\n\nIt is important to have a history as it helps us to see the child through a wider window than we would be able to if we were just observing the child.\n\nStep 2 - Comprehensive Child Development Assessment\n\nSeveral developmental domains are covered in our assessment reflecting how the child's functioning in these domains will affect his or her daily life.\n\nThese may include:\n\nCommunication skills\nSpeech and language skills development\nAttention and concentration\nLearning abilities\nBehaviour\nEmotional development\nSocial interaction\nSensory processing\nMotor skills\nEveryday living skills, adaptive skills\n\nThis approach is organized and will help to determine strengths and needs for further support. [2]\n\nStep 3 - Understanding Your Child's Development Profile\n\nAfter the assessment, we discuss our observations with parents in a simple and understandable way.\n\n\nThis includes:\n\nAreas where development is progressing well\nAreas that may need additional support\nDevelopmental priorities\nPractical guidance for home and school\nRecommendations for the next steps\n\nOur target is to support parents to have a clear understanding of their child's development and be confident in their decisions.\n\nThe Neuro-Ayurveda Development System\n\nAt Manovaidya, child development is seen through the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System, a framework which is structured to gain a perspective beyond the individual symptoms and consider the factors which influence a child's development.\n\nThis approach takes a whole-brained view of brain health, nutrition, sensory processing, behaviour, learning and experiences in daily life and how these areas relate to healthy development, rather than just a focus on the diagnosis. [4][6]\n\n1. Brain Nourishment System\n\nLearning, memory, communication, attention and emotional regulation are supported by healthy brain development. This pillar includes knowledge of factors that can affect healthy brain function and age appropriate development. [1]\n\n2. Gut Response System\n\nChildren's nutrition and digestion are important factors in their wellbeing. This pillar takes into account the importance of gut health in relation to nutrition and digestion, and views them as part of a child's developmental profile. [4]\n\n3. Neural Network System\n\nNew skills and abilities are acquired by children as a result of learning, repetition and meaningful experiences. This pillar emphasizes enhancing communication, learning, attention and cognitive functioning by providing appropriate developmental guidance. [1]\n\n\n4. Sensory Integration System\n\nChildren see, smell, taste, hear, and touch the world around them. This pillar will support parents in developing an awareness of the impact sensory processing can have on learning, behaviour, emotional regulation and participation in daily activities.\n\n5. Behaviour Guidance System\n\nChild behaviours are frequently a means of communication for needs, feelings or developmental issues. Unlike focusing just on changing behaviour, this pillar aims to teach parents about their children's behaviour patterns while encouraging positive strategies of behaviour guidance that will facilitate emotional development, confidence and healthy development.\n\nHow We Support Children\n\nChildren are unique and there is no universal program or process for child development.\nManovaidya's support is individualised to reflect the developmental profile, strengths and needs of each child.\n\nOur method involves guidance in areas such as:\n\nChild development assessment\nParent education\nBehaviour guidance strategies\nDaily routine planning\nLifestyle recommendations\nNutrition and healthy habits\nEmotional development\nCommunication support\nLearning support\nProgress monitoring and follow-up\n\nWe want to equip parents with understanding and guidance that they can use over the years and throughout their child's development, and be there alongside.\n\n\n\nWhy Parents Across India Choose Manovaidya\n\nOur child-centred and structured approach towards development makes Manovaidya a trusted institute for families from Delhi, Noida, Gurgaon, Faridabad, Ghaziabad and other parts of India.\n\nWe specialize in the following aspects of teaching, and these are what parents are attracted to:\n\nComprehensive Child Development Assessment\nPersonalised Development Guidance\nNeuro-Ayurveda Development System\nParent Education & Family Involvement\nHolistic Understanding of Brain, Behaviour & Development\nOnline & In-Clinic Consultations\nLong-Term Development Monitoring\n\nParents of children from Delhi, Noida, Gurgaon, Faridabad, Ghaziabad and other parts of India come to Manovaidya for Child Development Assessments, Developmental Guidance, Autism Treatment, ADHD Treatment and holistic child development care both online and at the clinic.\n\nWe want to promote the development of all children to their potential and to provide parents with the clarity, confidence and support they need during the journey of development.\n\n\n\nAbout Dr. Ankush Garg\n\n\nAutism, ADHD & Child Development Specialist in India\n\nDr. Ankush Garg is the Founder of Manovaidya and the developer of the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System.\n\nHis work focuses on understanding child development through a holistic perspective that combines brain development, behaviour, communication, learning, nutrition, sensory processing and emotional wellbeing.\n\nThrough structured assessments, personalised guidance and continuous follow-up, Dr. Ankush Garg helps families better understand their child's developmental needs and create practical strategies that support healthy growth, learning and everyday functioning.\n\nBased in Delhi NCR, he provides child development consultations for families across India through both online and in-clinic appointments.\n\nBased in Delhi NCR, Dr. Ankush Garg provides Child Development Assessments, Autism & ADHD guidance and holistic developmental support for children across India through the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System.\n\n\n\nFrequently Asked Questions (FAQs)\nWhat is child development?\n\nChild development refers to how children physically, emotionally, socially, mentally and behaviourally grow and develop from birth until adolescence. It contains communication, learning, movement, emotional regulation, social interaction and skills of daily living. Children's development varies from person to person and though there are general guidelines to developmental milestones these need to be used as a guide. [1][2]\nWhat are developmental milestones?\n\nDevelopmental skills are age appropriate skills that most children acquire as they mature. These benchmarks cover areas of speech, movement, learning, social interaction, emotional development and daily living skills. They are useful to parents and health professionals to track a child's growth across all domains, although there are small differences in growth rates between children. [1][2]\nWhen should I be concerned about my child's development?\n\nProfessional assessment should be considered if difficulties in speech, learning, movement, behaviour, attention, social interaction, and/or emotional development are persistent and are not developing as expected, or if the child has lost skills he or she previously had. The assessment is not a diagnosis based on a single symptom, but is to determine the child's strengths and developmental needs. [2]\nIs every child expected to develop at the same pace?\n\nNo. All children grow at their own rate. A few children will reach milestones sooner and others will take longer. Typical variation is small differences. Any delays or concerns that have a negative impact on daily functioning should be checked by a trained health-care provider, however. [1][2]\nWhat are the most common child development concerns?\n\nSpeech and language delay, Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD), Attention-Deficit/Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), learning problems, behavioural problems, sensory processing problems, motor delay, emotional regulation problems, and/or social communication problems are often reported concerns. [2][3]\nWhat are the early signs of developmental delay?\n\nSome of the potential early signs include delayed speech, lack of eye contact, not responding to name, limited social interaction, delayed walking, not following instructions, ongoing behavioral issues or loss of skills previously learned. These symptoms are not diagnostic but should be reviewed with a health care professional. [2][3]\nWhat is a child development assessment?\n\nA child development assessment is a systematic examination of various developmental domains including communication, learning, behaviour, attention, motor skills, emotional development, social interaction and daily living skills. The aim is to gain a profile of a child's developmental strengths and weaknesses and to determine where further support might be needed. [2]\nAt what age should a child development assessment be done?\n\nAn assessment of development may be considered when the parent, teacher or health professional(s) identify ongoing developmental issues. Assessment in the early years can frequently lead to better opportunities to learn about children's strengths and their developmental needs. [1][2]\nWhat causes developmental delays in children?\n\nDevelopmental differences can be caused by a variety of factors such as genetic differences, neurological differences, low birth weight, hearing or sight impairment, medical conditions or environmental factors. For many children there is no single cause and a full assessment is required to determine the child's developmental profile.\nHow important is early intervention for child development?\n\nChildren have a very rapid brain maturation process and if they are identified early and supported appropriately, they can become stronger in communication, learning, social/ adaptive skills. The kind of support advised will rely on every kid's distinct requirements and ought to be advised by qualified professionals. [1][2]\nHow can parents support their child's development at home?\n\nParents can help with development by communicating with their children, reading to them, offering play opportunities, maintaining healthy habits, giving children sufficient sleep and food, reducing excessive screen time and providing positive reinforcement and following advice from professionals if concerns are raised around development. [1]\nHow does Manovaidya support child development?\n\nAt Manovaidya, the first step in supporting a child is to have an understanding of the child's overall developmental profile, not just a single symptom. We provide parents with structured assessments, personalized guidance and the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System to help better understand communication, learning, behaviour, sensory processing, lifestyle factors and child development.\nWhat is the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System?\n\nThe Neuro-Ayurveda Development System is Manovaidya's 5-pillar structured approach which takes into account all the pillars of Brain Nourishment, Gut Response, Neural Network Development, Sensory Integration and Behaviour Guidance and understands the overall development of the child. It aims to offer a holistic view and full developmental assessment and individual guidance. [4][6]\nCan children improve with the right support?\n\nChildren make good developmental progress when they are given timely, individualised and appropriate support. Children's progress is different and may be influenced by their age, developmental profile, underlying conditions, family involvement and consistency of intervention. There is no certainty of a specific result. [1][2]\nWhen should I seek urgent medical attention for my child?\n\nIf your child has any kind of medical emergency, such as a seizure, loss of consciousness, serious injury, severe shortness of breath, acute weakness, abrupt loss of skills that they previously had or any other medical emergency, seek immediate medical attention. If you have developmental issues, but they are not considered an emergency, contact a health care provider for an evaluation.\nCan developmental concerns affect school performance?\n\nYes. Problems with communication, attention, learning, behaviour and emotional regulation can impact on learning, social interaction with peers and teachers, and participation in the classroom. Early assessment can aid in determining if there are areas where further education or development support is needed. [2]\nCan nutrition and sleep influence child development?\n\nGood nutrition and good sleep are related to good overall growth, brain development, learning, emotional regulation and physical wellbeing. Parents should talk to their child's health care provider if they are worried about their child's food, sleep or growth. [1]\nDo all developmental delays mean Autism or ADHD?\n\nNot all developmental delays are associated with ADHD or Autism Spectrum Disorder and there are many possible reasons for developmental delays. It is important to do a full developmental evaluation to get to the bottom of things and to ascertain the best way to guide the child. [2][3]\nHow often should a child's development be monitored?\n\nChild development should be assessed regularly using routine health assessments, developmental screenings, observations in the school environment and feedback from parents. Periodic follow-up assessments are used to monitor progress and make adjustments to recommendations, if necessary, when a child is receiving developmental support. [2]\nWhy should parents choose Manovaidya for child development care?\n\nAt Manovaidya, we take time to get to know the whole journey of each child's development. Structured assessments, tailored guidance and the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System provide parents with insight into their child's strengths, developmental needs and actionable recommendations. We use a holistic approach to child development care, parent education and long-term care for child development which is individualized to the child's profile.\n\n\n\nBook a Child Development Assessment\n\nConcerned About Your Child's Development?\n\nIf you have noticed differences in your child's communication, learning, behaviour, attention, social interaction or developmental milestones, understanding these concerns early is the first step towards providing the right support. [1][2]\n\nAt Manovaidya, we help parents understand their child's unique developmental profile through structured assessment, personalised guidance and the Neuro-Ayurveda Development System.\n\nLet's Understand Your Child's Development Together\n\nEvery child deserves the opportunity to grow, learn and thrive at their own pace.\n\nWhether your concerns relate to speech, behaviour, learning, attention, autism, ADHD or general developmental milestones, our team is here to support you with compassionate, evidence-informed and personalised guidance.\n\nBook a structured Child Development Assessment with Manovaidya to better understand your child's communication, learning, behaviour, attention and overall developmental needs.";
const refsRaw = "References\n[1] World Health Organization (WHO) — Improving Early Childhood Development: WHO Guideline\nhttps://www.who.int/publications/i/item/97892400020986\n[2] American Academy of Pediatrics (AAP) — Promoting Optimal Development: Developmental Surveillance and Screening\nhttps://doi.org/10.1542/peds.2019-3449\n[3] World Health Organization (WHO) — Autism Spectrum Disorders\nhttps://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/autism-spectrum-disorders\n[4] National Library of Medicine (PMC) — Neurodevelopmental Disorders Associated with Gut Microbiome Dysbiosis in Children\nhttps://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11275248/\n[5] National Library of Medicine (PubMed) — Ayurveda and Autism Spectrum Disorder: Clinical Research\nhttps://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=Ayurveda+autism+spectrum+disorder+children\n[6] AYUSH Research Portal — Ayurveda Research on Child Health & Developmental Conditions\nhttps://ayushportal.nic.in/";

